<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Aleksander Kakowski</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1862–1938</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Aleksander Kakowski was a Polish Catholic prelate, Archbishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw">Warsaw</a> from 1913 and a cardinal from 1919. In 1917–18 he was one of the three members of the Regency Council of the German- and Austrian-sponsored <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-poland">Kingdom of Poland</a>, which in November 1918 handed power to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jozef-pilsudski">Józef Piłsudski</a>. He remained Archbishop of Warsaw until his death in 1938.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/aleksander-kakowski.jpg" alt="Aleksander Kakowski" width="320" height="477" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aleksander_Kakowski.PNG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
